{"type":"standard","title":"Shumashti language","displaytitle":"<span lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><span class=\"mw-page-title-main\">Shumashti language</span></span>","namespace":{"id":0,"text":""},"wikibase_item":"Q33777","titles":{"canonical":"Shumashti_language","normalized":"Shumashti language","display":"<span lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><span class=\"mw-page-title-main\">Shumashti language</span></span>"},"pageid":5564420,"thumbnail":{"source":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Shumashti.svg/330px-Shumashti.svg.png","width":330,"height":319},"originalimage":{"source":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Shumashti.svg/1280px-Shumashti.svg.png","width":1184,"height":1145},"lang":"en","dir":"ltr","revision":"1265920064","tid":"733ef696-c5b6-11ef-9ad7-a3f30856c805","timestamp":"2024-12-29T07:28:08Z","description":"Indo-Aryan language of Afghanistan","description_source":"local","content_urls":{"desktop":{"page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shumashti_language","revisions":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shumashti_language?action=history","edit":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shumashti_language?action=edit","talk":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talk:Shumashti_language"},"mobile":{"page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shumashti_language","revisions":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:History/Shumashti_language","edit":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shumashti_language?action=edit","talk":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talk:Shumashti_language"}},"extract":"Shumashti – also known as Shumasht – is an Indo-Aryan language spoken in eastern Afghanistan. It is spoken in parts of Kunar Province: on the western side of the Kunar Valley between Jalalabad and the Pech Valley. The number of speakers was estimated at 1,000 in 1994.","extract_html":"<p><b>Shumashti</b> – also known as <b>Shumasht</b> – is an Indo-Aryan language spoken in eastern Afghanistan. It is spoken in parts of Kunar Province: on the western side of the Kunar Valley between Jalalabad and the Pech Valley. The number of speakers was estimated at 1,000 in 1994.</p>"}